<article class="env-lemma" id="0EW8">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0EW8">Lemma <span data-tag="0EW8">59.103.1</span>.</a> With notation as above. Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a sheaf on $S_{\acute{e}tale}$. The rule </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  (\mathit{Sch}/S)_ h \longrightarrow \textit{Sets},\quad (f : X \to S) \longmapsto \Gamma (X, f_{small}^{-1}\mathcal{F})  \]
</div>
  <p> is a sheaf and a fortiori a sheaf on $(\mathit{Sch}/S)_{\acute{e}tale}$. In fact this sheaf is equal to $\pi _ S^{-1}\mathcal{F}$ on $(\mathit{Sch}/S)_{\acute{e}tale}$ and $\epsilon _ S^{-1}\pi _ S^{-1}\mathcal{F}$ on $(\mathit{Sch}/S)_ h$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The statement about the étale topology is the content of Lemma <a href="/tag/09XN" data-tag="09XN">59.39.2</a>. To finish the proof it suffices to show that $\pi _ S^{-1}\mathcal{F}$ is a sheaf for the h topology. However, in Lemma <a href="/tag/0DDW" data-tag="0DDW">59.102.1</a> we have shown that $\pi _ S^{-1}\mathcal{F}$ is a sheaf even in the stronger ph topology. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>